        <li><strong>Know your keys:</strong> Primary, Foreign, Composite, Candidate, Alternate, and Super keys appear repeatedly in both MCQ and Short/long forms. This is the highest-return DBMS subtopic.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't neglect MS Access objects:</strong> Table, Query, Form, and Report are tested nearly every year, often in MCQs but also in Long Question sub-parts.</li>
        <li><strong>For Long Questions, practice writing code under time pressure:</strong> At least one Long Question sub-part almost always requires writing a complete C program (e.g., switch-case, factorial, binary search).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout success">
        <strong>Bottom Line:</strong> The BIEK CS Paper-II has settled into a <strong>highly predictable structure</strong>.
        While the specific questions change each year, the format, topic distribution, and question-type
        proportions have been remarkably stable from 2021 onward. This predictability is a strategic
        advantage that both teachers and students should leverage.
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 6 ═══════════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
    <h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

    <h3>Key Findings</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>The BIEK CS exam has a fixed structural core:</strong> 15 MCQs and 3 Long Questions
            are invariant across every year in the dataset. This suggests an institutional policy or
            examination board regulation that sets these counts.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>All variation is in the Short Question count:</strong> Changes from 27 → 36 → 33
            total questions are entirely attributable to Short Question adjustments. No other question
            type has ever varied.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The exam has stabilized post-2020:</strong> After the 2020 disruption, the format
            converged to 33 questions (15+15+3) and has held steady for 5 consecutive years — the
            longest period of format stability in the dataset.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Two knowledge pillars dominate:</strong> C Programming and Database Management
            Systems account for the overwhelming majority of question content, with roughly equal
            representation. Within these, control structures, functions, keys, and MS Access objects
            are the most frequently tested sub-topics.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The question bank is Broad but shallow:</strong> With {total_questions} questions across
            10 years, the dataset covers many topics but with limited depth per topic — making it
            suitable for pattern analysis but not for fine-grained sub-topic trend detection.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Limitations &amp; Caveats</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Missing 2020 data:</strong> The absence of the 2020 examination year creates a
            discontinuity in the trend line. We cannot determine whether the 2021 format (33 questions)
            was introduced during or after the pandemic-induced disruption, or whether 2020 followed
            the 2019 pattern (36 questions).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No distinction between annual and supplementary papers:</strong> The dataset does
            not encode whether a given year's questions come from the first annual examination or
            the supplementary (retake) examination. If both are present in some years, this could
            inflate question counts for those years.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Dataset size:</strong> With {total_questions} total rows and only {len(years_list)}
            year-level data points, statistical tests for trend significance have limited power.
            The findings should be interpreted as descriptive patterns, not inferential conclusions.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Community-sourced data:</strong> Questions were manually transcribed from scanned
            papers, so minor transcription errors cannot be ruled out. The non-standard <code>$</code>
            delimiter used for MCQ options and sub-parts required custom parsing.
        </li>
        <li>
